package test:guest;

/// The stake pool: delegation in exchange for a fungible stake unit, and
/// the two lifecycle facts the beacon's control plane consumes.
///
/// A fact rather than a request: the delegation has already moved by the
/// time the event exists, emission survives only a completed outcome, and
/// the warrant is that this package's code produced it. The beacon reads a
/// record; it is not being asked for anything.
///
/// Neither method names the pool it concerns. The kernel stamps an event's
/// emitter from the invocation it is running, so the pool a fact is about
/// is the instance that emitted it — a subject no guest can choose, and
/// therefore one no instance can claim for another. What a payload carries
/// is only what the emitter cannot already say: the amount.
world staking {
    import hyperscale:kernel/state;
    import hyperscale:kernel/events;

    use hyperscale:kernel/state.{delta-cell};

    /// Take a delegation into the pool's vault and return the stake units
    /// it mints for it.
    export stake: func(vault: borrow<delta-cell>, amount: list<u8>) -> list<u8>;

    /// Burn returned stake units and record the delegation leaving.
    ///
    /// The units are consumed and the pool's unbonding total grows by what
    /// they represent; releasing the staked resource to the delegator is a
    /// later leg this pool does not yet have.
    export unstake: func(unbonding: borrow<delta-cell>, amount: list<u8>);
}
